{{also|abàter}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abatere}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|abate|er|id2=agent noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbeɪ.tɚ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abater.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪtə(ɹ)|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who, or that which, [[abate]]s. {{defdate|From 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1583|translator={{w|Robert Parry (poet)|Robert Parry}}|title=The Second Part of the Myrror of Knighthood|author=Pedro de la Sierra|location=London|publisher=Thomas Este|section=Book 1, Part 2, Chapter 21|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A08548.0001.001
|passage=This is the great Prince of Grecia, called the Knight of the Sunne, restorer of the auncient kingdome of Tinacria, & the '''abater''' and breaker of the strength of the most strongest Giants in all the world.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1732|author=w:John Arbuthnot|title=Practical Rules of Diet in the Various Constitutions and Diseases of Human Bodies|location=London|publisher=J. Tonson|section=Chapter 1, section 26, p. 281|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004889406.0001.000
|passage=Anodyne, or '''Abaters''' of Pain of the Alimentary Kind. Such things as relax the Tension of the affected nervous Fibres {{...}}}}
#* '''1908''', John H. Wallace, ''Preservation of the Game, Fish and Forests of Alabama'', address given before the Alabama Press Association, 23 July, 1908, State Printers and Binders, p.{{nbsp}}6,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100772675]</sup>
#*: As a fever germ '''abater''' in a malarial district, a flock of bull-bats is worth a grove of quinine trees.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author=David I. Cook; David F. Van Haverbeke|chapter=Trees and shrubs can curb noise, but with quite a few loud ‘ifs’|title=The Yearbook of Agriculture, 972|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=US Department of Agriculture|page=28|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/100772675
|passage={{...}} research is proving the effectiveness of trees and shrubs as noise '''abaters'''—research prompted by the growing awareness that excessive noise is a form of environmental pollution.}}

